\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Example}
\title{A moving prime separates two topologies}

\p{Use the hyperbolic plane #{Q(x,y)=xy} and the integral orthogonal
references of the example in \ref{fcap-004F}. Enumerate the rational
primes without repetition as #{p_1,p_2,\ldots}. Define #{x_n\in O_f(U)}
by

##{(x_n)_p=\begin{cases}
\operatorname{diag}(p_n,p_n^{-1})&p=p_n,\\
1&p\ne p_n.
\end{cases}}

The exceptional diagonal matrix preserves #{xy}, and every other
component is the identity. Thus #{x_n} belongs to the principal
stage #{O_{\{p_n\}}(U)}.}

\p{For each fixed prime #{p}, the coordinate #{(x_n)_p} is the
identity for all but at most one #{n}. Hence #{x_n\to1} in the
topology inherited from the unrestricted product #{\prod_pO_p}.
Explicitly, a basic product neighborhood of the identity restricts
only a finite set #{F} of coordinates. Once the moving prime has
passed every prime in #{F}, all those coordinates equal the identity,
so the tuple belongs to that neighborhood.}

\p{In contrast, #{x_n\notin K(U)=\prod_pU_p^O} for every #{n}:
the entry #{p_n^{-1}} is not in #{\mathbb Z_{p_n}}, so its matrix
does not preserve the standard integral lattice. Since #{K(U)} is
an open identity neighborhood in the restricted-product topology,
the sequence does not converge to the identity there. Coordinate
continuity in \ref{fcap-004F} gives one topology inclusion; this
sequence proves that the restricted-product topology is strictly
finer in this example.}

\p{For the classical open-stage topology, see
\citet{Section 4 opening, p. 9}{conrad2011adelic} and its
finite-stage formula in Theorem 3.6. The calculation above isolates
what that topology detects: the nonintegral coordinate keeps moving,
although every fixed coordinate eventually becomes the identity.}
